>> 1) There seem to be lots of powerfull artefacts (Excalibur, Stormbringer, ...)
>>    only I've never seen one. How many of this artefacts are there, what do
>>    they do and how do you get them?
>
>
>   A few of the powerful artifacts are, as you said, Excalibur, Stormbringer,
>	Cleaver, Snickersnee, Mjolnir, and others.
>	  Excalibur is a blessed lawful longsword.  It is rustproof.
>	  Stormbringer is a cahaotic broadsword that draws the life out of 
>	    the opponent.
>	  Cleaver is a chaotic axe.(Basically, it just does extra damage)
>	  Snickersnee is a lawful katana.  Again, extra damage.
>	  Mjolnir is a lawful warhammer.  Damage.  I'm not sure about the rest.

	Okay, pretty good but not totally accurate.  Excalibur does
approximately 3X the damage of a longsword, and protects you from
level drain.  So does Stormy, but with the additional bonus of
draining levels on those things that you hit.  Cleaver and Grimtooth
basically suck, no need to use them unless you have a cruddy weapon.
Snickersnee and Mjollnir are excellent - Mjollnir does electrical
damage.  Most of the other weapons are used against a specific type of
monster (Ogresmasher, Dragonbane, GiantSlayer, Orcrist...)


>	  The best way to get them is by sacrificing many things to your god on
>	    an altar.  Another way is to wish for them.

	Correct.


>> 2) Sacrifices seem to offer some reward, but it never worked while I tried it,
>>    do you have to scrify tons of corpses before s.th. happens?

>	   It depends on how lucky you are and how much your god likes you, but
>	     yes, usually you need to sacrifice many dead monsters, the
>	     bigger the better.

>> 3) Does naming a single object have any effect, except for the fun of having
>>    a sword named slayer-of-dozends or crusher-of-worlds?
>
>	No, naming an item does not bestow upon it any special powers other 
>	  than a cool name.

	Not true!  A two-handed sword may be named Orcrist, and an
elven dagger (large runed knife) may be named Sting...Both bestowing
power against orcs and their ilk.  Sting will also glow bluish when
orcs are near.

>> 4) Are there positive results for dipping an object into a fountain?
>>    The only effect I had so far was a rusted sword. :(
>
>	  Yes, by dipping a longsword into fountain, many times a hand will
>	    reach up from the murky depths to bless it and you will recieve
>	    Excalibur.  Also, if you dip a cused worn item into a fountain,
>	    sometimes it will become uncursed.

	You must be over level five to happen, and it will be blessed
and rustproof.  
	There is a small chance that you can get a wish from a water
demon, too.


